[Music]  if you or someone you know is  experiencing rotator cuff pain  intermountain healthcare the sponsors of  this next segment are here to help  through exercises and sports medicine  here's how  my name is dr brian davis and we are  here at intermountain healthcare we are  in the sunset our beautiful office over  here at southern hills hospital  i am a physical medicine and  rehabilitation specialist my job is to  work with folks for non-surgical needs  of  their joint issues and so as we get  older the likelihood of us developing  rotator cuff issues goes up dramatically  when we're younger a rotator cuff  functions better unfortunately over time  as we continue to use our shoulders in  ways that are different than our  four-legged cousins we end up putting  greater demands on those rotator cuffs  over time what happens is that the cuff  begins to fail there is less flexibility  within the tendon the tendon has loss of  its normal blood flow which doesn't  allow for proper healing and we end up  going down this cascade of pain and  dysfunction and tendon damage that  unfortunately can lead to the point  where an individual may not be able to  reach overhead rotator cuff disease can  affect younger individuals but in those  circumstances it's usually because of  overuse injuries they might have played  a sport like a racket sport or a  throwing sport and that may have set  them up to have rotator cuff issues and  still again those don't tend to happen  until our 30s and our 40s and beyond but  as we get into our 50s and 60s and  beyond that's where we're much more  likely to develop those rotator cuff  issues that are going to be much more  problematic and plaguing for us  the most important thing from the  perspective of a clinician like myself  is first of all to try to prevent these  from happening in the first place which  means that you have to have the proper  exercise program you have to have the  proper training or trainer to help you  or you have to just be able to  evaluate your own fitness and make  certain that you're doing the right  exercises that are going to help  maintain your posture help maintain your  fitness and your general strength and  flexibility so one of the most important  things that we'll use in our instruction  for our patients is something as simple  as using a rubber band and what's nice  about these you can stick them in  between a door or have somebody hold  them for you and they create the  resistance so that you can work on  muscle strength  another thing that i actually use  throughout the course of this last 12 to  18 months during the pandemic are bands  to work on strength and other fitness  working on your postural muscles you can  use it to stick it over a door you can  stick it under the door next to it and  then you can create that resistance that  you're looking for  and as we move into that older age group  hopefully we age in gracefully but if we  develop a rotator cuff issue that's  where someone like myself would step in  and say all right here's what we need to  do in terms of the exercise protocols  here's how we have to work on your  posture and here's what we're going to  do to try to rehab this so we don't have  to go down that surgical pathway  thank you intermountain health  healthcare for sponsoring this segment  for more information just head to  intermountainhealthcare.org

